# AETHER — Builder / Canvas Deep Dive ("The Loom")

The canvas is the product. Everything else exists to feed it, save it, monetize it, or run it. This doc specs the drag-and-drop mechanics in detail — modeled on n8n/Figma-style infinite canvases, adapted for a layered trading-agent architecture.

---

## 1. Canvas fundamentals

- **Infinite pan/zoom surface**, grid background, snap-to-grid toggle
- **Pan**: space + drag, or middle-mouse drag, or two-finger trackpad scroll
- **Zoom**: scroll wheel / pinch, with zoom-percentage indicator and "fit to screen" button
- **Minimap**: bottom-right corner, shows full graph + current viewport, click-to-jump
- **Multi-select**: marquee (click-drag on empty canvas) or shift-click; supports group move, group delete, group disable
- **Alignment helpers**: snap guides when dragging a node near another's edge; "Align" / "Distribute" options in the multi-select toolbar

## 2. Layer bands

Nodes are visually grouped into **colored, translucent horizontal/vertical bands**, each labeled (e.g. "LAYER I — DATA COLLECTION," "LAYER IX — RISK MANAGEMENT") — a direct visual callback to the original architecture diagram's color-coded layer system. This is a genuinely useful UX pattern, not just decoration: it lets a user instantly see which stage of the pipeline they're editing and immediately spot an empty/misconfigured layer.

- Bands auto-expand to fit their contents
- A band can be **collapsed** (click the label) to hide its nodes and show just a compact summary chip (e.g. "3 nodes, 2 enabled")
- The left sidebar has a matching **"Layer visibility"** checklist to toggle whole bands on/off at once

## 3. Nodes

Every node represents one component (a data source, a model, a risk rule, an execution action, a memory system, etc.)

**Anatomy of a node:**
- Icon + name + layer-color accent border
- Input port(s) on the left, output port(s) on the right (typed — see §5)
- An **on/off toggle switch** in the node's header — this is the literal "enable/disable" mechanic you asked for
- A small **status dot**: grey (disabled), green (enabled, valid), amber (enabled, needs config), red (error)
- A **lock badge** in place of the toggle if the node is plan-gated and not yet unlocked

**Node states:**
| State | Visual |
|---|---|
| Enabled, valid | Solid border, green dot, normal color |
| Disabled | Greyed out, dashed outline, connections to it become dashed |
| Locked (paywalled) | Padlock icon overlay, greyed, clicking opens the Unlock modal instead of the config panel |
| Needs configuration | Amber dot, warning icon, tooltip lists missing fields |
| Error (during a run) | Red border, pulsing, tooltip shows the error message |
| Active (during a live run) | Subtle pulse/glow animation while actively processing |

**Node interactions:**
- **Click** → opens the right-hand Node Inspector panel
- **Double-click** → same as click, but also focuses the first config field
- **Drag** → move on canvas
- **Right-click** → context menu: Duplicate, Disable/Enable, Delete, View docs, Save as mini-preset
- **Drag from a port** → starts drawing a connection

## 4. Component Library (left sidebar)

- Search box at top
- Tabs/accordion sections matching the layers (Data Collection, Feature Engineering, Intelligence Agents, ML Prediction, Reinforcement Learning, Debate Layer, Confidence Engine, Risk Management, Execution, Trade Monitoring, Self-Learning, Memory)
- Each entry is a **draggable card**: icon, name, one-line description, lock badge + unlock price if gated
- Hover → tooltip with fuller description, inputs/outputs, and a "Learn more" link to `/app/library/:componentId`
- Drag a card onto the canvas to instantiate a node; it auto-snaps into the correct layer band based on its type

## 5. Connections & the type system

- Drag from an output port to a compatible input port to connect
- Ports are **typed** (e.g. `MarketData`, `NewsFeed`, `FeatureVector`, `Signal`, `RiskDecision`, `ExecutionOrder`, `TradeOutcome`) — the canvas visually indicates compatible ports (highlighted) while dragging a connection, and simply refuses an incompatible drop with an inline toast explaining why
- **Structural validation rules** (examples, not exhaustive):
  - Execution nodes cannot connect directly to raw Data Collection nodes — a Risk Management node must sit between them
  - A Self-Learning node needs at least one Trade Monitoring output as input to have anything to learn from
  - Memory nodes can connect to multiple consumers (fan-out allowed); most other nodes are single-input
- Disabled nodes' connections render as dashed/greyed lines and are excluded from any run
- **"Validate Bot"** button in the toolbar runs the full rule set and lists every problem in the bottom Console panel with click-to-jump-to-node links

## 6. Node Inspector (right panel)

Appears when a node is selected. Contents vary per node type but always include:
- Node name (editable) and description
- On/off toggle (mirrors the node header toggle)
- All configuration fields for that component — examples:
  - **News Source node**: checkboxes for which channels/APIs to pull from, API key field, refresh interval slider
  - **Risk Gate node**: threshold sliders, explicit false-positive vs. false-negative cost weighting, max position size
  - **Self-Learning node**: sub-module toggles (post-mortem writer / rule clustering / model reweighting), retraining frequency
  - **Execution node**: which venue/API, order type, slippage tolerance
- "Remove from canvas" button (with confirmation if the node has connections)

## 7. Paywall interaction

- Clicking a locked node opens an **Unlock modal**: component name, what it does, its price (one-time / subscription-included / credit cost), and an embedded Razorpay checkout — no full-page redirect, so the user never loses their place on the canvas
- On successful payment, the modal closes, the node's lock badge is replaced by the normal toggle, and a toast confirms "Unlocked — {component} is ready to configure"
- If the user cancels/fails payment, the node stays locked with no interruption to the rest of their work

## 8. Sub-flows / mini-presets

- A user can select a connected group of nodes (e.g. "my custom risk gate: 2 nodes + their connection") and **"Save as mini-preset"**
- Mini-presets appear in a personal section of the Component Library ("My saved blocks") and can be dragged into any other bot as a single unit, then expanded/edited
- This is what makes "share your preset with other users" meaningful at a sub-component level, not just whole-bot level

## 9. Toolbar reference

| Button | Action |
|---|---|
| Bot name (inline editable) | Rename |
| Save | Saves current graph state (autosave also runs periodically) |
| Save as Preset ▾ | Save as new preset / update existing preset |
| Version history | Dropdown list of saved versions, click to preview, "Restore" button |
| Undo / Redo | Standard graph-edit history |
| Validate | Runs structural rule checks, opens Console with results |
| Templates | Quick-insert common sub-graphs (e.g. "standard debate layer," "basic risk gate") |
| Share | Generates a link, sets visibility (Private/Unlisted/Public) |
| Run Backtest | Navigates to/opens the backtest config panel pre-filled with this bot |
| Go Live (gated) | Starts paper-trading (or, on the verified real-fund tier, real) execution |
| Zoom controls | +, −, fit-to-screen, percentage display |

## 10. Empty & first-time states

- Blank canvas shows a light watermark hint: "Drag a component from the left to get started" with an arrow pointing at the library panel
- If a user tries to Run Backtest on an incomplete graph, they get a friendly blocking modal listing exactly what's missing (not just "invalid graph")

## 11. Performance/UX notes worth deciding early

- Large graphs (50+ nodes) need virtualization so the canvas doesn't lag — likely only relevant once the platform matures past MVP
- Autosave + explicit "unsaved changes" indicator with a browser-navigation guard, so users never lose work
- Keyboard shortcuts: Delete (remove selected), Cmd/Ctrl+D (duplicate), Cmd/Ctrl+Z/Shift+Z (undo/redo), Cmd/Ctrl+S (save), Space+drag (pan)